00:29:41.000 --> 00:31:02.000
Juan offered the families of his victims an apology in court, maintaining that he never intended to hurt their loved ones. In contrast, the prosecution argued that Juan Alvarez had no intention of taking his own life. Rather, he wanted to create a grand spectacle that would get his wife's attention and pity, so she would take him back. There were elements about Juan's story that didn't add up to evidence at the scene. Juan claimed that he had poured gasoline on himself and his vehicle, with the initial intention to set himself alight. His clothing from that day tested positive for traces of gasoline, but an amount that arson investigators attributed to splashback when pouring it all over his vehicle, not directly over himself. Witnesses who had interacted with Juan immediately after the crash noted that he didn't smell of gasoline. Doctors who had treated him didn't recall Juan's eyes or skin as being red, inflamed, or irritated, tell tale signs of prolonged exposure to gasoline.
胡安在法庭上向受害者家属道歉，坚称他从未打算伤害他们所爱的人。相比之下，检方辩称胡安·阿尔瓦雷斯根本没有结束自己生命的意图。相反，他想制造一场盛大的奇观，以引起妻子的注意和怜悯，这样她就会回心转意。胡安的故事中有些要素与现场证据不符。胡安声称他把汽油倒在自己和车上，最初打算自焚。他那天的衣服检测出有汽油痕迹，但纵火调查人员认为这是在把汽油倒在车上时溅到的，而不是直接倒在自己身上。事故发生后立即与胡安接触的目击者指出，他身上没有汽油味。治疗他的医生不记得胡安的眼睛或皮肤有发红、发炎或刺激的迹象，这些都是长期接触汽油的明显迹象。

32
00:31:02.000 --> 00:32:31.000
The prosecution insisted that Juan didn't make the bona fide attempt to take his own life, as he alleged. Furthermore, an expert witness revealed that fire damage on the jeep's parking brake assembly indicated that it was activated at the time of impact. This meant the car was in park, which contradicted Juan's claims that he had made considerable effort to try and move the jeep to avoid a collision. Witnesses reported that only the car's passenger door was open at the time it was demolished, which contradicted Juan's claims that he had thrown himself from the driver's side seconds before impact. Additionally, the vehicle's headlights were off, indicating Juan didn't intend for it to be seen until it was too late. There was no debate that the gasoline Juan had poured over his jeep was a contributing factor in starting the blaze that consumed Metrolink 100's front passenger carriage. The arson count was crucial to Juan's case. The felony statute on arson in California meant that if Juan was found guilty of causing the fire, he was to be held liable for the murders, even if he didn't intend to kill anyone.
检方坚持认为胡安并没有像他声称的那样进行真正的自杀尝试。此外，一位专家证人透露，吉普车驻车制动装置上的火灾损坏表明它在撞击时已启动。这意味着车处于停车档，这与胡安声称他曾努力尝试移动吉普车以避免碰撞的说法相矛盾。目击者报告说，在车被撞毁时，只有乘客侧车门是开着的，这与胡安声称他在撞击前几秒钟从驾驶侧跳出的说法相矛盾。此外，车辆的车灯是关着的，表明胡安不打算让它被看到，直到为时已晚。毫无疑问，胡安倒在吉普车上的汽油是引发大火并吞噬Metrolink 100次列车前客车车厢的一个促成因素。纵火罪名对胡安的案件至关重要。加州的纵火重罪法规意味着，如果胡安被判纵火罪成立，即使他无意杀人，也要对谋杀负责。

33
00:32:31.000 --> 00:33:56.000
Even the defense's expert witness agreed with most of the prosecution's findings. They conceded that if a person poured gasoline over their vehicle and waited for a train to collide with it, then they probably intended for a fire to start, using the train as an ignition source. After three hours of deliberation, the jury found Juan Alvarez guilty of one count of arson and of all 11 counts of first-degree murder with special circumstances. They found him not guilty of train wrecking, unsatisfied that he intended to derail the train. Nevertheless, the guilty verdict ensured Juan would face a possible death sentence. In talking to the Daily News, three members of the jury said they likely would not have convicted Juan Alvarez of murder had it not been for the felony statute on arson. The jury foreman said he was reckless, he's a dangerous man, but I don't think he had any intention. The week prior to the verdict, the jury inspected four of the train cars damaged in the collision.
甚至辩方的专家证人也同意检方的大部分调查结果。他们承认，如果一个人把汽油倒在车上并等待火车撞上它，那么他们很可能打算引发火灾，利用火车作为点火源。经过三个小时的审议，陪审团裁定胡安·阿尔瓦雷斯一项纵火罪和所有11项带有特殊情况的一级谋杀罪成立。他们裁定他火车破坏罪不成立，认为没有证据表明他蓄意使火车脱轨。尽管如此，有罪判决确保胡安将面临可能的死刑。在与《每日新闻》交谈时，三名陪审员表示，如果不是因为纵火重罪法规，他们可能不会判胡安·阿尔瓦雷斯谋杀罪。陪审团领班说，他很鲁莽，他是一个危险的人，但我不认为他有任何意图。在判决前一周，陪审团检查了碰撞中损坏的四节火车车厢。

34
00:33:56.000 --> 00:35:17.000
Rather than implicate Juan Alvarez, the wreckage had the opposite effect. The jury foreman told the Daily News that seeing the wreckage reaffirmed his belief that Juan did not expect anyone to get injured. Quote, seeing how big those trains were and how heavy they were, I really don't think a reasonable person would expect a train to derail by parking their car on the tracks. In the weeks following the trial, the jury opted to remove the death penalty as a possible sentence, reasoning that Juan Alvarez never intended to kill. Juan's wife and cousin were featured in the LA Times pleading for leniency, citing that he belonged in a mental institution, not on death row. They regretted not getting Juan the psychiatric help he needed, saying they believed the catastrophe could have been avoided if they had. The Superior Court judge was far more skeptical of Juan Alvarez. He didn't believe that Juan was unaware that his actions would have dire consequences for others.
这些残骸非但没有牵连胡安·阿尔瓦雷斯，反而产生了相反的效果。陪审团领班告诉《每日新闻》，看到残骸重申了他的信念，即胡安没有预料到会有人受伤。“看到那些火车有多大、多重，我真的不认为一个理性的人会预料到把车停在铁轨上会导致火车脱轨。”审判后的几周里，陪审团选择取消死刑作为可能的判决，理由是胡安·阿尔瓦雷斯从未打算杀人。胡安的妻子和表弟在《洛杉矶时报》上恳求宽大处理，称他应该被关进精神病院，而不是死囚牢房。他们后悔没有让胡安得到他需要的精神病治疗，并表示他们相信如果他们这样做了，这场灾难本可以避免。高等法院法官对胡安·阿尔瓦雷斯持怀疑态度。他不相信胡安不知道他的行为会给他人带来可怕的后果。

35
00:35:17.000 --> 00:36:18.000
He told Alvarez that the phone message he left for his cousin following the crash seemed set up, like somebody playing a part, and that it sounded like a very bad actor trying to convince the person that he really feels as he says he does, and it doesn't ring true. The judge told Juan, I don't believe for a minute you intended to kill yourself or harm yourself in any way. I think you were setting up a scenario so you could go back to your family. If there was a sentence known as forever in state prison, I would give it to you. What I'm going to do is almost the equivalent, and I agree that these 11 people who died and their families deserve a maximum sentence in this case. Juan was given 11 consecutive life sentences.
他告诉阿尔瓦雷斯，他在事故后给表弟留下的电话留言似乎是预先安排好的，就像有人在演戏，听起来像是一个非常糟糕的演员试图说服对方他真的像他说的那样感受，这听起来不真实。法官告诉胡安：“我一点也不相信你打算自杀或以任何方式伤害自己。我认为你是在设置一个场景，这样你就可以回到你的家人身边。如果有一种判决叫在州立监狱里永远待下去，我会给你的。我要做的是几乎等同的判决，我同意这11名死者和他们的家人在这个案件中应该得到最高刑罚。”胡安被判处11个连续的终身监禁。

36
00:36:18.000 --> 00:37:51.000
It was the maximum sentence that could be awarded given that the death penalty had been taken off the table. Juan Alvarez appealed his conviction in 2012, arguing in part that the prosecution failed to prove a connection between the arson he committed and the deaths of the victims. His appeal failed. A number of the survivors of the Glendale train crash filed a civil lawsuit against Metrolink, claiming that the tragedy could have been avoided, had the engineer of Metrolink 100, Bruce Gray, followed protocol. During the trial, Bruce testified that in the seconds before the crash, he saw what appeared to be a reflection coming from a vehicle about three-quarters of a mile ahead. Bruce said he became fixated on the area, and when he saw an outline of the jeep, he hit the train's emergency brakes. However, the black box taken from the train wreckage revealed that the service brakes had only been applied initially, with a few critical seconds passing before the emergency brakes were activated. The attorney representing the victims argued that had Bruce Gray applied the brakes when he said he had a trial, the crash could have been avoided.
这是在死刑被排除后所能判处的最高刑罚。胡安·阿尔瓦雷斯在2012年对他的定罪提出上诉，部分理由是检方未能证明他所犯的纵火罪与受害者死亡之间的联系。他的上诉失败了。格伦代尔火车事故的一些幸存者对Metrolink提起了民事诉讼，声称如果Metrolink 100次列车的工程师布鲁斯·格雷遵循规程，这场悲剧本可以避免。在审判期间，布鲁斯作证说，在撞车前几秒钟，他看到前方约四分之三英里处有一辆车发出的反射光。布鲁斯说他开始盯着那个区域，当他看到吉普车的轮廓时，他踩下了火车的紧急制动器。然而，从火车残骸中取出的黑匣子显示，行车制动器最初只是被应用，在紧急制动器启动前经过了几个关键的秒数。代表受害者的律师辩称，如果布鲁斯·格雷在审判时所说的那样踩下刹车，撞车事故本可以避免。

37
00:37:51.000 --> 00:39:43.000
Metrolink settled the lawsuit, with a $39 million combined payout. Following the Glendale train crash, Metrolink's fleet underwent changes, with the spokesman claiming that it was now the safest commuter rail line in the country. Their trains were fitted out with crash energy management technology that included bumpers designed to absorb the lion's share of energy from a collision. 11 unrelated fates were sealed when Juan Alvarez abandoned his jeep on the Ventura line near Glendale train station on January 26, 2005. Manuel Alcalá aged 51, Julia Bennett aged 44, Alfonso Caballero aged 62, Elizabeth Hill aged 65, Scott McCown aged 42, William Perrin aged 53, James Totino aged 47, and Don Wiley aged 58 lost their lives aboard Metrolink 100. Leonard Romero aged 53, Henry Kalinsky aged 39, and the train's engineer, Thomas Ormiston aged 58, died on Metrolink 901. Thomas had worked on trains since 1972. His job was often a topic of conversation whenever he dropped his car off to his mechanic and friend, Walter. During one visit, the pair spoke of the possibility of a train crash occurring after reading about similar incidents in the papers.
Metrolink以3900万美元的赔偿金达成了和解。格伦代尔火车事故后，Metrolink的车队进行了更换，发言人声称它现在是全国最安全的通勤铁路线。他们的火车配备了碰撞能量管理技术，包括旨在吸收碰撞中大部分能量的保险杠。2005年1月26日，当胡安·阿尔瓦雷斯在格伦代尔火车站附近的文图拉线（Ventura line）上遗弃他的吉普车时，11个不相关的命运被封存了。51岁的曼努埃尔·阿尔卡拉（Manuel Alcalá）、44岁的朱莉娅·贝内特（Julia Bennett）、62岁的阿方索·卡巴列罗（Alfonso Caballero）、65岁的伊丽莎白·希尔（Elizabeth Hill）、42岁的斯科特·麦考恩（Scott McCown）、53岁的威廉·佩林（William Perrin）、47岁的詹姆斯·托蒂诺（James Totino）和58岁的唐·威利（Don Wiley）在Metrolink 100次列车上丧生。53岁的伦纳德·罗梅罗（Leonard Romero）、39岁的亨利·卡林斯基（Henry Kalinsky）和列车工程师、58岁的托马斯·奥米斯顿（Thomas Ormiston）死于Metrolink 901次列车。托马斯自1972年以来一直从事火车工作。每当他把车送到他的机械师兼朋友沃尔特（Walter）那里时，他的工作经常成为谈话的话题。在一次访问中，两人在报纸上读到类似事件后，谈到了发生火车事故的可能性。

38
00:39:43.000 --> 00:39:48.000
